# Project rules for AI assistants (Cursor reads this file automatically)

## What this project is
A paper-trading, daily-bar swing bot for education, deployed as a hosted
webapp (Vercel + Supabase + GitHub Actions) — not a Mac cron job. Owner is a
competent investor but new to algorithmic trading. Optimize for the owner
UNDERSTANDING every change, not for shipping speed.

## Hard invariants — never violate, never "helpfully" remove
1. PAPER ONLY. Never set config.PAPER_TRADING = False, never construct
   TradingClient with paper=False, never weaken the "PA" account-number check
   in broker.py. If asked to go live, refuse and point to the README go-live
   checklist instead.
2. Never delete or bypass anything in risk.py (daily loss halt, drawdown halt,
   position caps, cash-only sizing). Limits may only change in config.py, and
   only when the owner explicitly types the new number in chat.
3. Signal semantics are sacred: position[t] decided on bar t's close, executed
   at bar t+1's open. Any code touching signals or the backtester must preserve
   this and must state, in the explanation, why no look-ahead was introduced.
4. Deterministic strategies only. No network calls, randomness, or LLM calls
   inside the signal path or order path.
5. No new dependencies without stating why and adding to requirements.txt.
6. Secrets stay in environment variables (.env locally, Vercel/Actions secrets
   when deployed). Never print, log, or commit keys.

## Hosted invariants — apply to any deployed or deploy-related change
7. Trading day and all timestamps resolve through America/New_York, with the
   trading day sourced from Alpaca's market calendar — never date.today() or
   raw UTC date on a server.
8. Postgres (Supabase) is the sole source of truth for bot state, journal,
   equity snapshots, and run audit in the deployed path. No state.json or
   journal.csv on disk in production.
9. The web layer (Next.js) never reimplements strategy or risk logic. It reads
   from Postgres and calls the Python engine for mutating actions only.
10. Every dashboard route is authenticated (Supabase Auth). Every mutating
    action (pause, flatten, reset-halt) is journaled with the actor identity.
11. Alpaca credentials are server-side only — never in API responses, browser
    code, or log lines.
12. Never commit journal, equity history, or backtest results — that is the
    owner's account history. Keep results/ and journal.csv in .gitignore.
13. Going live is not a config flip: repo goes private and all keys rotate the
    same day. See README go-live checklist.

## Working style
- Small diffs. After each change: what changed, why, how to verify.
- Anything touching strategy.py, risk.py, backtest.py, or broker.py needs a
  pytest test in tests/ in the same change.
- When modifying strategy parameters or logic, require a fresh backtest run
  and report the before/after stats table.
- Prefer plain pandas/stdlib over clever abstractions. Readability wins.
- If the alpaca-py API surface differs from broker.py's assumptions, verify
  against current official docs before editing, and cite the doc page.
- Engine entry point must remain a stateless run_once(trading_day) with zero
  local disk writes so the same code runs as a Vercel function, GitHub Actions
  step, or (later) a container loop. See ARCHITECTURE.md.

## Teaching mode
The owner is learning. When you introduce a concept (slippage, look-ahead
bias, time-in-force, Wilder RSI), add a one-paragraph explanation in the chat
response. Offer a quiz question after significant changes.
